\chapter{Bindings, shadowing, and scope}
\label{ch:9}
\index{Bindings, shadowing, and scope}

Use \OnxCodeTerm{let} for an immutable binding and \OnxCodeTerm{var} for local mutable state. A binding belongs to its lexical scope. An inner declaration can shadow a previous name, and a closure that already resolved the earlier binding keeps that lexical identity. Names inside a destructuring initializer are resolved before the new pattern introduces its leaves. Changing a spelling in one scope can change which binding a closure captures.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn main()
{
  let limit = 42;
  {
    let limit = 7;
    assert(limit == 7);
  }
  assert(limit == 42);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

A mutable loop counter is a local state location. A shadowed immutable name is a fresh binding. The checker maintains this distinction through type resolution and C name generation. It also constrains what may be captured and what may escape its scope. See \cref{ch:12} for immutable captures and \cref{ch:27} for the identity of a checked loan over a named place.

\section{Rename the bindings on paper}
In the repository's shadow example the inner declaration multiplies a
value named \OnxCodeTerm{n} by two and gives the result the same name.
Put a subscript on each declaration as you read it. First $n_1$ receives
21. At the multiplication there is no $n_2$ yet, so its operand is
$n_1$. The product 42 becomes $n_2$, and the print inside the block
reads that binding. Crossing the closing brace removes $n_2$ from
lookup. The next print reads $n_1$ and displays 21. Nothing had to save
21 on entry to the block or write 21 back on exit.

Change the outer declaration to \OnxCodeTerm{var} and remove the inner
\OnxCodeTerm{let}, leaving an assignment in its place. Now there is just
one subscript in your trace. Both prints should display 42. Removing the
inner declaration without making the outer one mutable should instead
produce a diagnostic at the assignment. Choosing between these three
programs is a question about how many bindings you intend, rather than
whether the compiler reuses a machine storage location. The same method
works when the source name denotes a function: mark the declaration that
is in scope at the call before trying to predict which body runs.

